  "issue_number": 3291,
  "issue_title": "`make_conditional` has a lot of problems",
  "issue_body": "It handles both evaluating preconditions and applying ranges. It does not match the order and logic in RFC9110 section 13 and 14. It looks like some of this confusion was due to trying to add range handling in to the existing conditional handling method, making the API a lot more complicated.\n\nSome of the problems, and problems revealed in other parts of the code:\n\n- Only applies to `GET` and `HEAD`, but conditional responses should apply to mutating methods as well.\n- Processes range requests first, then conditional requests. This is backwards.\n- Does not ensure the ETag being compared is strong/weak depending on what's being checked.\n- `is_resource_modified` returns a bool, which is insufficient for the checks described in the spec. It needs to distinguish between 200, 304, 412, and 416. `make_conditional` tries to guess what status code to apply based on what `is_resource_modified` returns and what headers are set.\n- Does not validate directly that the requested range unit is `bytes`. Allows setting `Accept-Ranges` to something besides `bytes` even though it will only handle bytes.\n- Precedence of `If-Match` `If-Modified-Since` `If-Not-Match`, and `If-Unmodified-Since` is messed up.\n- `is_resource_modified` doesn't take `if_unmodified_since`, so that header is completely ignored.\n- `If-Range` date uses `<=` comparison instead of `==`.\n- Sets the `Date` header for some reason. The spec does not mention this. Comments that servers will overwrite this, but doesn't explain why that's wrong. The date set by the server will not be meaningfully different from the date set here. `to_wsgi_response` doesn't set it, so it's not otherwise in-scope within Werkzeug.\n- Similarly, sets `Content-Length`, but `to_wsgi_headers` already does that. But then again, `to_wsgi_headers` doesn't use `calculate_content_length`.\n- Doesn't raise on 412, only sets the status code. So 412 still contains the original response's body and headers. `to_wsgi_response` doesn't handle clearing this like it does for 304.\n- Invalid ranges produced 416, but that's only for valid but unsatisfiable ranges. Should return 200 for those.\n- Considered multiple ranges invalid (and incorrectly returned 416). Spec says can either choose to ignore and return 200, or use first range only.\n- Suffix range larger than the complete length was considered invalid instead of clamped.\n- `ETagSet.contains/_weak` assume the given value had the correct weak/strong marker. Now that `ETag` exists, that can be passed and checked directly.\n- `SharedDataMiddleware` tries to handle this again on its own, but only 304, doesn't support 412, 416, or ranges at all. Can use `send_file` instead.\n- If `SharedDataMiddleware` wants to use `send_file`, `send_file` needs to be updated to handle file objects better. It can figure out their length, and potentially other properties through `os` functions on its handle.\n\nThis can be split into two new methods on `Response`, `apply_conditions(request)` and `apply_range(request)`. `make_conditional`, related helper methods, and `is_resource_modified`, can be deprecated.",
  "merged_at": "2026-09-27T17:19:56Z",
